Create a refined minimalist editorial poster that turns a geological or hydrological structure into the main composition.

Use clean warm ivory fine-art paper and one large mineral [COLOR FIELD] with subtle silkscreen, gouache and pigment texture. Build a strong [GEOGRAPHIC STRUCTURE] into the color field — such as a diagonal fault, sweeping reservoir arc, concentric excavation, or merging currents.

Keep the structure bold and readable from a distance, while adding extremely small [MICRO SCENE] details in black or charcoal to create scale and narrative.

Set “[TITLE]” in sophisticated editorial typography and add:

“[SUBTITLE]”

Include a few small, meaningful [FIELD DATA] notes related to the subject, plus one natural handwritten annotation:

“[HANDWRITTEN NOTE]”

The hierarchy should feel graphic from far away and archival up close: large color field, strong geographic structure, tiny survey scene, refined typography and micro field notes.

